{"name":"Chapter","hex":"#9f9369","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chapter","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the main sections into which a published work is divided, especially a book.","A section of a work, a collection of works, or fragments of works, often manuscripts or transcriptions, created by scholars or advocates, not the original authors, to aid in finding portions of the texts."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To divide into chapters.","To put into a chapter."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chapter"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chapter (books)","blocks":[{"text":"A chapter is any of the main thematic divisions within a writing of relative length, such as a book of prose, poetry, or law. A book with chapters may have multiple chapters that respectively comprise discrete topics or themes. In each case, chapters can be numbered, titled, or both. An example of a chapter that has become well known is \"Down the Rabbit-Hole\", which is the first chapter from Alice's Adventures in Wonderland."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Sahih_Al-Bukhari_in_English.png/330px-Sahih_Al-Bukhari_in_English.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapter_(books)","wikidata":"Q1980247"},{"title":"Chapter book","blocks":[{"text":"A chapter book is a story book intended for intermediate readers, generally age 7–10. Unlike picture books for beginning readers, a chapter book tells the story primarily through prose rather than pictures. Unlike books for advanced readers, chapter books contain plentiful illustrations. The name refers to the fact that the stories are usually divided into short chapters, which provide readers with opportunities to stop and resume reading if their attention spans are not long enough to finish the book in one sitting. Chapter books are usually works of fiction of moderate length and complexity."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chapter_book","wikidata":"Q5073531"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/The_wood_beyond_the_world_by_William_Morris_%28cropped%29.jpg/250px-The_wood_beyond_the_world_by_William_Morris_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/be/The_wood_beyond_the_world_by_William_Morris_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/be/The_wood_beyond_the_world_by_William_Morris_%28cropped%29.jpg/250px-The_wood_beyond_the_world_by_William_Morris_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The opening page of The Wood Beyond the World (1894) by William Morris. The chapter title is at the top, in red text.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_wood_beyond_the_world_by_William_Morris_(cropped).jpg"}],"sounds":{"chapter":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/En-us-chapter.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/1/12/En-us-chapter.ogg/En-us-chapter.ogg.mp3"},{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/ba/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-chapter.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/ba/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-chapter.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-chapter.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/b/ba/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-chapter.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-chapter.wav.ogg"}]},"pictures":[{"title":"Sala Capitular, Cated